---
/**
 * hospitality / LocationBK — "Villa Marindu"'s LOCATION band.
 *
 * ROLE: the getting-here section. The design draws a two-part head (eyebrow +
 * the address line set as the display headline on the left, the arrival note on
 * the right, baseline-aligned and wrapping), then a 1.2fr / .8fr grid: a plotted
 * map face at 360px beside a bordered house-rules ledger. Reproduced verbatim —
 * the 46px plot grid, the two brass roads at 48% / 42%, the pin translated to
 * sit ON the crossing, the 26px gutter, the 28px 30px card padding, the 13px 0
 * rows on a --rule-soft hairline. Nothing here is re-derived: every colour is a
 * PageBK token whose value is the design's own hex.
 *
 * EDITABLE HOOKS (every one is declared in components/editor/genericContentSchema.ts):
 *   location.tag                        — the brass eyebrow
 *   location.headline                   — the h2. set:html, so an owner's <em>
 *                                         picks up the template's italic-brass
 *                                         emphasis (styled by PageBK, not here).
 *   location.sub                        — the arrival note, right of the head
 *   location.rules.<original i>.label    — ledger row label
 *   location.rules.<original i>.value    — ledger row value
 *   location.lat / location.lng          — feed the Leaflet boot; no data-field,
 *                                         they are coordinates, not copy.
 *
 * DERIVED, printed but not editable HERE (the hook for each lives in the field
 * that owns it, so putting a second one on this section would be a duplicate
 * input fighting the first):
 *   layout.businessName  → the pin bubble label
 *   layout.contact.city  → the map caption (the design's "villaRegion")
 *   layout.contact.address / .lat / .lng → fallbacks behind the location.* pair
 *
 * THE HEADLINE IS THE ADDRESS, and there is no second address line. The design's
 * head is eyebrow + h2 + sub, with the h2 set to `{{ addressLine }}` — three
 * elements, no fourth.
 *
 * This file briefly printed the address as a paragraph beneath the h2, on the
 * reasoning that lib/derive-content-defaults.ts fills location.headline with
 * "Where to find us" on every site while location.address arrives holding the
 * real street line, so mapping the design straight would drop the address off
 * the page. That reasoning was sound and the answer was still wrong: the head is
 * a flex row whose two cells share a bottom edge, so a two-line paragraph in the
 * left cell drags the sub down a row and strands it on the right beside nothing.
 * The design has no such paragraph and neither does this.
 *
 * The address still reaches a visitor. It feeds the geocode below — tier 2 of
 * the map, which is how this section finds its own pin — and FooterBK prints it
 * in the contact column, so location.address keeps a live sidebar input. An
 * owner who wants the street line as the heading pastes it into the headline,
 * which is exactly what the design does.
 *
 * NOT RENDERED, deliberately:
 *   location.phone and location.hours. The design's LOCATION prints neither; the
 *   closing band and the footer own the number. Drawing a tel: row here would be
 *   a control this design does not have, and this section is not the gate on
 *   them — see the gate note below.
 *   location.directions.text/href. Declared in the schema, but the design has no
 *   directions control in this band and R2 forbids inventing one.
 *   No onPage() anchor-remap helper. It exists to rewrite in-page hrefs onto this
 *   page's ids, and this section emits NO href at all — the map is not a link,
 *   the ledger is not a link. An unused PAGE_ANCHORS set would be dead code.
 *   LocationBJ omits it for the same reason.
 *
 * SECTION GATE. Body content only: the map (which needs real coordinates OR a
 * real address behind it), the ledger rows, or the arrival paragraph. NOT the
 * tag and NOT the headline — derive-content-defaults fills both on every site,
 * so a gate that counted them would ship an eyebrow and a heading over an empty
 * grid. The grid itself is gated separately, so a site with only a sub keeps its
 * paragraph and grows no empty two-column hole under it.
 *
 * THE MAP, three tiers, mirroring LocationBJ:
 *   1. build-time lat/lng → window.__hospitalityBKInitMap(lat, lng, label)
 *   2. else any address   → client-side Nominatim lookup, then the same boot
 *   3. else               → the drawn plot-grid face, which IS the design
 * The drawn face lives INSIDE #map until Leaflet mounts; PageBK's boot does
 * el.innerHTML = '' first, which is what erases it. The literal id="map" and the
 * exact global name __hospitalityBKInitMap are a contract with PageBK — rename
 * either and the drawn face silently stays on screen under the tiles.
 * PageBK loads leaflet.css and leaflet.js and defines the boot. It once defined
 * the boot WITHOUT the two tags, so window.L never existed, the boot returned on
 * its first line and tier 3 shipped on every villa however many coordinates an
 * owner typed. scripts/check-template-blocks.mjs now fails that combination.
 *
 * A CLEARED coordinate field leaves '', which passes != null and which Number('')
 * turns into 0 — booting Leaflet and pinning the villa at 0,0 in the Gulf of
 * Guinea with its name on the marker: a map asserting a location the owner never
 * gave. num() rejects blank and out-of-range values so a cleared field falls
 * through to the address lookup instead, and uses ?? rather than || so a genuine
 * 0 survives.
 *
 * ACCESSIBILITY. The map cell carries NO role="img" and NO aria-label. The only
 * name available would be a sentence I wrote ("Map showing …"), which is exactly
 * the component-supplied copy R2/R9 forbid, and the cell holds no upload — it is
 * an illustration of a map. So the whole drawn face, pin and bubble included, is
 * aria-hidden and the information it pictures is announced as real text beside
 * it: the address line and the ledger. The caption is the one piece of that
 * frame that is NOT decoration (it is the owner's city), so it sits OUTSIDE #map
 * and outside the aria-hidden face, and survives the Leaflet boot.
 *
 * TWO DEVIATIONS FROM THE DESIGN, both forced, both small:
 *   1. The pin bubble drops white-space: nowrap. The frame clips overflow, so a
 *      long business name would be sliced off at BOTH ends; wrapping beats
 *      truncating on the element that names the property.
 *   2. The caption gains an opaque --night backing. In the design it sits on the
 *      drawn face; the moment Leaflet paints tiles underneath, unbacked 10px
 *      tracked caps are unreadable. Same colour pair the design already ships
 *      (--muted on --night, 4.34:1 — the design's own value, see PageBK).
 */
interface Props { content: any; layout: any; }
const { content, layout } = Astro.props;
const loc = content.location || {};
const tag = loc.tag || '';
const headline = loc.headline || '';
const sub = loc.sub || '';
// READ, NOT PRINTED. The design's head is eyebrow + h2 + sub, and its h2 IS the
// address — `{{ addressLine }}`. There is no second address paragraph anywhere
// in that section. This template grew one so a site whose headline was not its
// address would still show the street line; that paragraph is also what pushed
// the sub down a row, because it joined the cell the sub bottom-aligns to.
//
// The address is not lost by dropping it. It feeds the geocode below (tier 2 of
// the map, which is how this very section finds the pin) and FooterBK prints it
// in the contact column — so location.address still owns a live sidebar input
// and an owner still reads their street line on the page.
const address = String(loc.address || layout.contact?.address || '').trim();

// Blank rows are dropped: the editor's "+ Add" inserts { label: '', value: '' },
// and an empty row would spend a hairline plus 26px of padding saying nothing.
// Every surviving row keeps its ORIGINAL index, so deleting row 2 cannot
// misdirect an edit of row 3 — and the filter is what makes the gate honest: a
// rules array of nothing but blanks no longer keeps the ledger alive.
type RuleRow = { label: string; value: string; i: number };
const rules: RuleRow[] = (Array.isArray(loc.rules) ? loc.rules : [])
  .map((r: any, i: number) => ({ label: String(r?.label ?? '').trim(), value: String(r?.value ?? '').trim(), i }))
  .filter((r: RuleRow) => Boolean(r.label || r.value));

const num = (v: any, max: number): number | null => {
  if (v === null || v === undefined || String(v).trim() === '') return null;
  const n = Number(v);
  return Number.isFinite(n) && Math.abs(n) <= max ? n : null;
};
const lat = num(loc.lat, 90) ?? num(layout.contact?.lat, 90);
const lng = num(loc.lng, 180) ?? num(layout.contact?.lng, 180);
const hasCoords = lat !== null && lng !== null;
const hasAddress = !!address;
// The drawn face is a picture claiming where this property physically is. With
// neither coordinates nor an address there is nothing real behind that claim, so
// the whole cell leaves and the ledger takes the full width.
const hasMap = hasCoords || hasAddress;

// Marker popup + pin bubble. No placeholder: with no business name the bubble
// leaves and the pin is a bare dot rather than a label reading "Your Business".
const brand = String(layout.businessName ?? '').trim();
// The design's "villaRegion". Nothing derives it, so on a site without a city it
// is simply absent rather than guessed out of the address.
const locality = String(layout.contact?.city ?? '').trim();

const hasGrid = hasMap || rules.length > 0;
const hasLoc = hasGrid || !!sub;
---
{hasLoc && (
<section class="loc" id="location">
  <div class="wrap">
    {/* TWO children, exactly as the design draws it: the heading block and the
        sub beside it. No address paragraph — the design has none, because its
        heading IS the address. See the note on `address` in the frontmatter for
        where the street line still appears. */}
    <div class="loc-head reveal">
      <div class="loc-head-l">
        {tag && <span class="kick" data-field="location.tag">{tag}</span>}
        {headline && <h2 class="h2 loc-h2" data-field="location.headline" set:html={headline}></h2>}
      </div>
      {sub && <p class="body loc-sub" data-field="location.sub">{sub}</p>}
    </div>
    {hasGrid && (
      <div class:list={['loc-grid', 'reveal', (!hasMap || rules.length === 0) && 'one-up']} data-delay="1">
        {hasMap && (
          <div class="map-frame">
            <div id="map">
              {/* Tier-3 only, and entirely decorative: PageBK's boot empties #map
                  before Leaflet draws, so tiles never stack on top of this. */}
              <div class="map-face" aria-hidden="true">
                <span class="road road-h"></span>
                <span class="road road-v"></span>
                <span class="pin">
                  <span class="dot"></span>
                  {brand && <span class="plabel">{brand}</span>}
                </span>
              </div>
            </div>
            {/* OUTSIDE #map on purpose: the city is real text, not fallback
                chrome, so it has to survive the boot that erases the face. */}
            {locality && <span class="mono map-cap">{locality}</span>}
          </div>
        )}
        {rules.length > 0 && (
          <div class="rules">
            {/* Both spans always render, even when one is blank: the row is
                space-between, so dropping an empty span would promote its
                sibling to the wrong edge. An empty span draws nothing — the
                guard that matters ran above, where a wholly blank row was
                dropped along with its hairline. */}
            {rules.map((r) => (
              <div class="rowrule rule">
                <span class="rk" data-field={`location.rules.${r.i}.label`}>{r.label}</span>
                <span class="rv" data-field={`location.rules.${r.i}.value`}>{r.value}</span>
              </div>
            ))}
          </div>
        )}
      </div>
    )}
  </div>
</section>
)}
{hasCoords && (
  <script is:inline define:vars={{ lat, lng, brand }}>
    (function () {
      // Bounded like the geocode branch below: this script is emitted where the
      // section sits, ABOVE the wrapper's boot, so one retry is the normal case —
      // but an unbounded loop would spin a timer forever on any page where the
      // wrapper script never ran.
      var attempt = 0;
      (function init() {
        if (window.__hospitalityBKInitMap) { window.__hospitalityBKInitMap(lat, lng, brand); }
        else if (attempt++ < 25) { setTimeout(init, 80); }
      })();
    })();
  </script>
)}
{!hasCoords && hasAddress && (
  <script is:inline define:vars={{ address, brand }}>
    (function geocodeThenInit() {
      var attempt = 0;
      function tryInit(la, ln) {
        if (window.__hospitalityBKInitMap) { window.__hospitalityBKInitMap(la, ln, brand); }
        else if (attempt++ < 25) { setTimeout(function () { tryInit(la, ln); }, 80); }
      }
      try {
        var query = encodeURIComponent(String(address).replace(/\n/g, ', '));
        var url = 'https://nominatim.openstreetmap.org/search?format=json&limit=1&q=' + query;
        fetch(url, { headers: { 'Accept-Language': 'en' } })
          .then(function (r) { return r.ok ? r.json() : []; })
          .then(function (results) {
            if (!Array.isArray(results) || results.length === 0) return;
            var la = parseFloat(results[0].lat);
            var ln = parseFloat(results[0].lon);
            if (isNaN(la) || isNaN(ln)) return;
            tryInit(la, ln);
          })
          .catch(function () { /* keep the drawn face: it is the design */ });
      } catch (e) { /* never block render */ }
    })();
  </script>
)}
<style>
  /* ── THE HEAD · flex, baseline-bottom, space-between, wrapping before either
        side can overflow. 36px to the grid, straight from the design.
        Two children only — the heading block and the sub. It briefly held a
        third, an address paragraph, and that is what dragged the sub down: in
        this row a taller left cell moves everything aligned to its bottom edge.
        The design has no such paragraph, so neither does this. */
  .loc-head {
    display: flex; align-items: flex-end; justify-content: space-between;
    gap: 30px; flex-wrap: wrap; margin: 0 0 36px;
  }
  .loc-head-l { min-width: 0; }
  /* PageBK's .h2 already carries the design's face, clamp(31px,4vw,54px)/1.1 and
     --bone-hi. Only the margin differs: the design pushes the h2 .26em BELOW the
     eyebrow and nothing after it. */
  .loc-h2 { margin: .26em 0 0; }
  /* Qualified with the parent class on purpose — PageBK's bare-paragraph reset
     is (0,1,1) and it sets a bottom margin this must not inherit. */
  .loc-head .loc-sub { margin: 0; min-width: 0; max-width: 36ch; overflow-wrap: anywhere; }

  /* ── THE SPLIT · the design's 1.2fr / .8fr with a 26px gutter. Either cell can
        be absent — no coordinates and no address drops the map, no rows drop the
        ledger — and the survivor takes the full width instead of leaving a hole
        beside itself. */
  .loc-grid {
    display: grid; grid-template-columns: minmax(0,1.2fr) minmax(0,.8fr);
    gap: 26px; align-items: stretch;
  }
  .loc-grid.one-up { grid-template-columns: minmax(0,1fr); }

  /* ── THE MAP CELL · panel ground inside a hairline, 3px corners, 360px. */
  .map-frame {
    position: relative; min-height: 360px; overflow: hidden;
    border-radius: var(--r-lg); border: 1px solid var(--rule);
    background-color: var(--night-2);
  }
  #map { position: absolute; inset: 0; z-index: 0; }
  /* The plotted face: a 46px brass grid with two roads crossing under the pin.
     Token-derived, so an admin accent pick moves the plot lines with the pin. */
  .map-face {
    position: absolute; inset: 0; background-color: var(--night-2);
    background-image:
      repeating-linear-gradient(0deg, rgba(var(--accent-rgb),.09) 0 1px, transparent 1px 46px),
      repeating-linear-gradient(90deg, rgba(var(--accent-rgb),.09) 0 1px, transparent 1px 46px);
  }
  .road { position: absolute; background-color: rgba(var(--accent-rgb),.24); }
  .road-h { top: 48%; left: 0; right: 0; height: 5px; }
  .road-v { top: 0; bottom: 0; left: 42%; width: 4px; }
  /* max-width sits on the ABSPOS pin, not on the bubble. The stack is CENTRED on
     the 42% crossing, so it only stays inside the frame when it is capped at twice
     the smaller side — 84% — less 12px of air each side. calc(100% - 24px) would
     still let a long property name run past the left edge and be clipped there by
     the frame, which is the truncation the wrapping bubble exists to avoid.
     width: max-content is required alongside it, or abspos shrink-to-fit
     collapses the stack to (containing block - left). */
  .pin {
    position: absolute; top: 48%; left: 42%; transform: translate(-50%,-100%);
    display: grid; justify-items: center; gap: 9px;
    width: max-content; max-width: calc(84% - 24px); text-align: center; z-index: 1;
  }
  .dot {
    width: 14px; height: 14px; border-radius: 50%;
    background-color: var(--accent); border: 3px solid var(--night);
  }
  /* Square, not var(--r-ctl): the design's pin bubble is the one label in this
     template with no radius at all. */
  .plabel {
    background-color: var(--accent); color: var(--on-accent);
    font-family: var(--sans); font-weight: 600; font-size: 9px;
    letter-spacing: .2em; text-transform: uppercase;
    padding: 5px 12px; text-align: center; overflow-wrap: anywhere;
  }
  /* .mono supplies the design's 10px / .22em / --muted; only the placement and
     the opaque backing are added here. */
  .map-cap {
    position: absolute; left: 18px; bottom: 18px; z-index: 2;
    background-color: var(--night); padding: 5px 10px; border-radius: var(--r-ctl);
    max-width: calc(100% - 36px); overflow-wrap: anywhere;
  }

  /* ── THE LEDGER · panel ground, hairline, 3px, 28px 30px. */
  .rules {
    background-color: var(--night-2); border: 1px solid var(--rule);
    border-radius: var(--r-lg); padding: 28px 30px;
  }
  /* .rowrule brings the --rule-soft hairline. The design draws it under EVERY
     row including the last, which is why there is no `+ .rule` trick here: the
     closing hairline above the card's 28px of padding is the design. */
  .rule { justify-content: space-between; gap: 16px; padding: 13px 0; }
  .rk { font-size: 13px; line-height: 1.5; color: var(--muted); min-width: 0; overflow-wrap: anywhere; }
  .rv { font-size: 14px; line-height: 1.5; color: var(--bone-hi); text-align: right; min-width: 0; overflow-wrap: anywhere; }

  /* ── RESPONSIVE · the wrapper owns the shared 760 / 520 pass; this is the
        section's own grid. */
  @media (max-width: 900px) {
    /* Ledger under the map: the rules are what a guest reads twice. */
    .loc-grid { grid-template-columns: minmax(0,1fr); }
    .loc-head .loc-sub { max-width: none; }
  }
  @media (max-width: 760px) {
    .map-frame { min-height: 300px; }
    .loc-head { margin-bottom: 28px; }
    .rules { padding: 22px 24px; }
  }
  @media (max-width: 520px) {
    .map-frame { min-height: 240px; }
    .rules { padding: 16px 20px; }
    /* Two lines rather than squeezing a policy value into a 90px column: label
       above, value below, both left-aligned. */
    .rule { flex-direction: column; align-items: flex-start; gap: 4px; padding: 12px 0; }
    .rv { text-align: left; }
    .map-cap { left: 12px; bottom: 12px; font-size: 9px; letter-spacing: .16em; max-width: calc(100% - 24px); }
    .plabel { font-size: 8.5px; letter-spacing: .14em; padding: 5px 10px; }
  }
</style>
